GNU patch for OpenVMS
=====================

This is GNU patch @VERSION@ built natively for OpenVMS @ARCH@, from the
unmodified GNU release plus the patches in the vms-patch project:

    https://github.com/issinoho/vms-patch

Kit version: @KITVERSION@.  GNU patch is free software under the GNU General
Public License, version 3 or later; see COPYING.  The manual page is
PATCH.TXT (plain text) and PATCH.1.

What is installed
-----------------
    PATCH$ROOT:[BIN]PATCH.EXE              patch
    PATCH$ROOT:[000000]PATCH$SETUP.COM     defines the patch command
    PATCH$ROOT:[DOC]                       this file, COPYING, NEWS,
                                           PATCH.TXT, PATCH.1
    SYS$STARTUP:PATCH$STARTUP.COM          defines PATCH$ROOT

Setting up
----------
The kit installs PATCH$STARTUP.COM into SYS$STARTUP and runs it once.  To
define PATCH$ROOT at every boot, the system manager adds this line to
SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM:

    $ @SYS$STARTUP:PATCH$STARTUP.COM

Each user (or SYLOGIN.COM, for everyone) then defines the command:

    $ @PATCH$ROOT:[000000]PATCH$SETUP.COM

Using patch
-----------
    $ patch "-p1" "-i" fix.diff
    $ patch "-R" "-i" fix.diff        ! undo it

patch reads unified and context diffs (diff -u, diff -c) and normal diffs.
File names in the diff are Unix-style names relative to the current
directory: src/main.c is [.SRC]MAIN.C.  Backups (-b) and rejects keep
patch's Unix names, main.c.orig and main.c.rej, which need an ODS-5 disk.

Not available on VMS
--------------------
- ed-style diffs (diff -e): patch runs the Unix ed editor for them.
- Getting files from RCS or SCCS before patching them.

Exit status
-----------
Under DCL a run with failed hunks (exit code 1) or trouble (2) exits with an
error-severity status, so ON ERROR and IF .NOT. $STATUS work; the exit code
N is ($STATUS .AND. %X7F8) / 8.  Under a GNV shell, $? is the exit code as
on Unix.

Upper-case options and batch jobs
---------------------------------
Under the TRADITIONAL DCL parse style DCL upper-cases the unquoted parts of
a command and the C run-time library passes them to patch in lower case, so
an unquoted -R (reverse) arrives as -r (reject file).  Batch jobs use
TRADITIONAL even when interactive logins are set to EXTENDED.  Quote the
options ("-R"), use the long ones (--reverse), or start the procedure with

    $ SET PROCESS/PARSE_STYLE=EXTENDED
